1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:10,360
The team did something reasonable. A
long-lived branch conflicted with main on the

2
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:14,389
same two lines of a configuration
file, at every rebase.

3
00:00:14,429 --> 00:00:19,353
A conflict is Git stopping because
both sides changed the same lines.

4
00:00:19,393 --> 00:00:24,749
Resolving the same one by hand for the
tenth time is how mistakes are made.

5
00:00:24,789 --> 00:00:26,548
Rerere removes that work.

6
00:00:26,888 --> 00:00:30,803
Then one day somebody resolved that
conflict in a hurry, and wrongly.

7
00:00:30,843 --> 00:00:35,388
From that moment Git wrote the wrong answer
into the file at every later rebase,

8
00:00:35,428 --> 00:00:38,540
with the same calm output it
prints for a right answer.

9
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:42,781
With one extra setting it also staged
it. A wrong value was one git rebase

10
00:00:42,821 --> 00:00:46,048
--continue away from being a
commit, and nobody typed it.

11
00:00:46,388 --> 00:00:51,167
Rerere replays a recorded resolution
whenever the same conflict text appears, a

12
00:00:51,207 --> 00:00:53,545
wrong one as faithfully as a right one.

13
00:00:55,385 --> 00:00:59,156
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:59,196 --> 00:01:04,215
You know conflicts from the merge module,
and you know that in a rebase the sides

15
00:01:04,255 --> 00:01:09,212
are swapped. Today you add a feature that
sits beside conflict resolution and is

16
00:01:09,252 --> 00:01:14,459
entirely local: it lives in .git/rr-cache,
which is never pushed, fetched or cloned.

17
00:01:14,799 --> 00:01:18,380
You'll watch one resolution being
recorded in a throw-away test merge.

18
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:23,435
Then the test merge is thrown away, and the
record is replayed in the real merge, in

19
00:01:23,475 --> 00:01:26,548
the opposite direction. And
replayed again in a rebase.

20
00:01:26,888 --> 00:01:31,799
Then the wrong-resolution case and its
repair. And last, a failure that the authors

21
00:01:31,839 --> 00:01:36,631
of this course ran into while writing the
chapter: a lock race between rerere and

22
00:01:36,671 --> 00:01:38,660
automatic maintenance on Git 2.55.

23
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:42,780
say what rerere records
and at which moment;

24
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:45,199
show a recorded resolution being replayed
in a later merge and in a rebase;

25
00:01:45,539 --> 00:01:48,872
forget a wrong recorded resolution
and record a correct one;

26
00:01:49,212 --> 00:01:52,388
explain why a replayed resolution
still has to be reviewed and tested;

27
00:01:52,728 --> 00:01:55,753
explain the lock failure on Git 2.55
and the setting that prevents it.

28
00:01:57,593 --> 00:02:01,595
In one sentence: with rerere.enabled set,
Git records the text of every conflict

29
00:02:01,635 --> 00:02:05,688
together with what you made of it, and
when a conflict with the same text appears

30
00:02:05,728 --> 00:02:09,579
again, in any merge, rebase, cherry-pick,
revert or git am -3, it writes your

31
00:02:09,619 --> 00:02:12,863
earlier resolution into the file
instead of the conflict markers.

32
00:02:13,203 --> 00:02:17,495
"Rerere" stands for "reuse recorded
resolution". The commands that can stop on a

33
00:02:17,535 --> 00:02:20,528
conflict call git rerere for
you, so you rarely type it.

34
00:02:20,568 --> 00:02:21,828
It works in two moments.

35
00:02:22,168 --> 00:02:26,975
Moment one: when a conflict appears. Git
normalizes the conflict in each file.

36
00:02:27,015 --> 00:02:32,196
Labels are stripped from the markers, the
two sides are sorted, and the base part of

37
00:02:32,236 --> 00:02:33,687
diff3 output is removed.

38
00:02:34,027 --> 00:02:38,821
It hashes the conflict hunks and uses
the hash as the name of a directory in

39
00:02:38,861 --> 00:02:43,973
.git/rr-cache. If there is no recorded
resolution there, it stores the normalized

40
00:02:44,013 --> 00:02:47,280
conflict as preimage and
prints "Recorded preimage".

41
00:02:47,620 --> 00:02:52,128
If there is one, it merges the old
conflict, the old resolution and the new

42
00:02:52,168 --> 00:02:56,857
conflict, and when that merge is clean
it writes the result into your file and

43
00:02:56,897 --> 00:02:59,767
prints "Resolved ... using
previous resolution".

44
00:03:00,107 --> 00:03:08,163
Moment two: when you conclude. At the
commit, or at git rebase --continue, Git

45
00:03:08,203 --> 00:03:15,531
stores the resolved file as postimage
and prints "Recorded resolution".

46
00:03:15,871 --> 00:03:20,631
The normalization is why one record serves
both directions of a merge, and a merge

47
00:03:20,671 --> 00:03:24,435
as well as a rebase. The name
depends on the two competing texts.

48
00:03:24,475 --> 00:03:28,415
It doesn't depend on branch names,
on who is "ours", or on the path.

49
00:03:28,755 --> 00:03:33,119
One property decides how safe the feature
is. Rerere leaves the index alone.

50
00:03:33,159 --> 00:03:37,812
A replayed file stays unmerged, with three
versions still in the index, until you

51
00:03:37,852 --> 00:03:40,419
git add it, unless
rerere.autoUpdate is true.

52
00:03:40,759 --> 00:03:47,067
Inside the dot git folder:
rr-cache/<conflict-id>/preimage and postimage.

53
00:03:47,107 --> 00:03:54,023
A file MERGE_RR, which maps the conflicted
paths of the operation in progress to

54
00:03:54,063 --> 00:04:01,153
conflict IDs. And, briefly, MERGE_RR.lock.
git rerere gc prunes unresolved records

55
00:04:01,193 --> 00:04:04,718
after 15 days and resolved ones after 60.

56
00:04:05,058 --> 00:04:10,434
When to use it: for people who rebase long
branches or rebuild integration branches.

57
00:04:10,474 --> 00:04:15,787
When not: don't turn on rerere.autoUpdate
unless tests run before every --continue.

58
00:04:15,827 --> 00:04:21,139
The stop with UU is the only review a replay
gets. And know the limits: records are

59
00:04:21,179 --> 00:04:26,297
per clone, they expire, and a conflict
whose text has changed is a new conflict.

60
00:04:28,137 --> 00:04:33,292
The textbook's analogy: a notebook next to
the merge desk, each page with a conflict

61
00:04:33,332 --> 00:04:38,177
on the left and your answer on the right.
When the same left side turns up, Git

62
00:04:38,217 --> 00:04:40,404
copies the right side into the file.

63
00:04:40,744 --> 00:04:45,478
The analogy breaks where the danger is.
The notebook matches text, not intent, and

64
00:04:45,518 --> 00:04:47,284
it never asks before it copies.

65
00:04:47,624 --> 00:04:51,944
Two refinements. The left side of each
page is written in a canonical form: no

66
00:04:51,984 --> 00:04:56,361
branch labels, the two sides in sorted
order. That is why the same page matches

67
00:04:56,401 --> 00:05:00,274
whichever branch you happen to be
on. And the notebook is yours alone.

68
00:05:00,314 --> 00:05:03,293
A colleague's clone has a
different notebook, or none.

69
00:05:05,133 --> 00:05:13,718
Try it now, on paper. Write the two
moments, and next to each, the file rerere

70
00:05:13,758 --> 00:05:20,131
writes. Pause me for thirty
seconds and write your answer.

71
00:05:24,971 --> 00:05:30,048
Column one writes the left page, the preimage.
Column two writes the right page, the

72
00:05:30,088 --> 00:05:35,044
postimage. Column three finds the same page
by its hash and copies the answer into

73
00:05:35,084 --> 00:05:39,979
the file. Read the last line of column
three twice: the file is resolved, and the

74
00:05:40,019 --> 00:05:41,441
index is still unmerged.

75
00:05:41,781 --> 00:05:47,944
One record, written in the test
merge and read in the real merge.

76
00:05:47,984 --> 00:05:52,142
In a moment you'll see
a rebase read it too.

77
00:05:56,482 --> 00:06:02,161
An ordinary conflict, plus one new line:
"Recorded preimage for 'retrieval.yaml'".

78
00:06:02,201 --> 00:06:03,975
Look at what was recorded.

79
00:06:07,315 --> 00:06:11,791
The directory name is the conflict ID.
The preimage has bare markers and the two

80
00:06:11,831 --> 00:06:16,082
sides in sorted order, top_k: 20 first,
although your file shows HEAD first.

81
00:06:16,122 --> 00:06:17,042
Resolve as usual.

82
00:06:17,382 --> 00:06:20,805
git rerere diff compares the
recorded conflict with your file.

83
00:06:20,845 --> 00:06:24,490
The commit at the end of the snippet
prints "Recorded resolution".

84
00:06:24,530 --> 00:06:26,892
The test merge has done
its job. Remove it.

85
00:06:30,232 --> 00:06:33,711
The merge is gone. The record
stays, with both halves.

86
00:06:33,751 --> 00:06:38,859
A week later the branch is merged into
main, in the opposite direction from the

87
00:06:38,899 --> 00:06:42,639
test. Predict the exit status.
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

88
00:06:49,479 --> 00:06:52,995
Read this output with care. The
merge still reports CONFLICT.

89
00:06:53,035 --> 00:06:57,017
It still says "Automatic merge
failed". It still exits with status 1.

90
00:06:57,057 --> 00:07:01,680
Only one line, "Resolved 'retrieval.yaml'
using previous resolution.", tells you

91
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:04,070
that the file no longer contains markers.

92
00:07:04,410 --> 00:07:09,429
The file holds your resolution. git status
says UU, and the index still has three

93
00:07:09,469 --> 00:07:12,864
stages, because rerere wrote
the file and nothing else.

94
00:07:12,904 --> 00:07:17,362
git rerere remaining prints nothing:
no path is left to resolve by hand.

95
00:07:17,402 --> 00:07:22,109
This stop is your review point. Read
the file, run the tests, then stage and

96
00:07:22,149 --> 00:07:22,671
conclude.

97
00:07:28,811 --> 00:07:33,450
The rebase stops as usual. In a rebase the
sides are swapped, and the normalization

98
00:07:33,490 --> 00:07:34,690
makes that irrelevant.

99
00:07:35,030 --> 00:07:38,669
UU, and a file without markers.
You add it and continue.

100
00:07:38,709 --> 00:07:42,086
Now with rerere.autoUpdate.
Quick quiz, two options.

101
00:07:42,126 --> 00:07:46,882
With auto-update on, the rebase still
stops. Or it runs straight through.

102
00:07:46,922 --> 00:07:47,933
Say it out loud.

103
00:07:52,773 --> 00:07:57,926
It still stops. "Staged 'retrieval.yaml'
using previous resolution."

104
00:07:57,966 --> 00:08:04,111
Only git rebase --continue is left to do.
Nothing forces you to look at the file.

105
00:08:04,451 --> 00:08:09,597
Apart from the file content, the output
is identical to the correct case.

106
00:08:09,637 --> 00:08:14,072
That's how a wrong value reaches
main without anyone typing it.

107
00:08:14,112 --> 00:08:20,038
The correction has three steps: make rerere
forget, bring the conflict back, resolve

108
00:08:20,078 --> 00:08:20,464
again.

109
00:08:20,804 --> 00:08:26,906
"Forgot resolution". The postimage is gone,
and the markers are back in the file.

110
00:08:27,246 --> 00:08:29,282
"Recorded resolution", and the
postimage now has top_k: 50.

111
00:08:29,322 --> 00:08:32,204
If the wrong merge is already committed and
not pushed: undo it first, merge again,

112
00:08:32,244 --> 00:08:35,160
and then forget. If it is pushed: fix forward
with a new commit and still forget, or

113
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:36,568
the next rebase brings the mistake back.

114
00:08:36,908 --> 00:08:42,189
Replay labs/run ch14c/rerere-lock. It
produces the failure on demand: an exec line

115
00:08:42,229 --> 00:08:47,445
of the rebase creates the lock file after
each replayed commit, as a stand-in for

116
00:08:47,485 --> 00:08:48,742
the background task.

117
00:08:57,082 --> 00:09:02,135
After the failure git rerere status prints
nothing: rerere never saw this conflict,

118
00:09:02,175 --> 00:09:07,229
so a resolution made now would not be
recorded. One manual git rerere repairs that:

119
00:09:07,269 --> 00:09:12,261
"Recorded preimage". Removing the lock file
by hand is right here only because the

120
00:09:12,301 --> 00:09:17,170
stand-in created it. The real background
task removes its own lock when it ends.

121
00:09:17,510 --> 00:09:22,666
How often does the real race occur?
Replay labs/run ch14c/rerere-lock-race.

122
00:09:22,706 --> 00:09:26,476
It runs the same rebase twenty
times per configuration.

123
00:09:42,116 --> 00:09:43,764
Five mistakes to watch for.

124
00:09:44,104 --> 00:09:47,977
Reading "CONFLICT" and exit status
1 as "rerere did not work".

125
00:09:48,017 --> 00:09:53,277
Root cause: a replay writes the file and
leaves the index unmerged, so the operation

126
00:09:53,317 --> 00:09:58,326
still stops; the line "Resolved ... using
previous resolution" is the only sign.

127
00:09:58,666 --> 00:10:03,121
Turning on rerere.autoUpdate to save a
step. Root cause: it stages the replayed

128
00:10:03,161 --> 00:10:07,104
file, which removes the UU stop that
is the only review a replay gets.

129
00:10:07,444 --> 00:10:11,696
Fixing a wrong replay in the file and
committing, without git rerere forget.

130
00:10:11,736 --> 00:10:16,440
Root cause: the wrong postimage is still
recorded for that conflict text and will be

131
00:10:16,480 --> 00:10:18,248
replayed at the next occurrence.

132
00:10:18,588 --> 00:10:24,547
Expecting a colleague's clone
to replay your resolutions.

133
00:10:24,587 --> 00:10:31,600
Root cause: rr-cache is local and
is not pushed, fetched or cloned.

134
00:10:31,940 --> 00:10:38,060
Treating the MERGE_RR.lock failure as
corruption. Root cause: a background rerere-gc

135
00:10:38,100 --> 00:10:42,826
task and the rebase wanted the
same lock; nothing is damaged, and

136
00:10:42,866 --> 00:10:45,833
maintenance.rerere-gc.auto=0 prevents it.

137
00:10:47,673 --> 00:10:53,300
Now, out of the lab. A retrieval team keeps
a reranking branch alive for six weeks.

138
00:10:53,340 --> 00:10:58,625
It conflicts with main on the same
configuration lines at every weekly rebase.

139
00:10:58,665 --> 00:11:04,223
The branch owner enables rerere in her
clone, sets maintenance.rerere-gc.auto to 0

140
00:11:04,263 --> 00:11:06,954
there, and leaves rerere.autoUpdate off.

141
00:11:07,294 --> 00:11:12,051
In week three she resolves the conflict
carelessly, keeping the old candidate count.

142
00:11:12,091 --> 00:11:16,620
In week four the rebase stops, prints
"using previous resolution", and shows UU.

143
00:11:16,660 --> 00:11:21,189
Because she treats that stop as a review
point, she reads the file, sees the old

144
00:11:21,229 --> 00:11:25,700
count next to the enabled reranker, and
runs the evaluation, which confirms the

145
00:11:25,740 --> 00:11:30,269
regression. She makes rerere forget the
path, restores the conflict, resolves it

146
00:11:30,309 --> 00:11:33,924
correctly, and continues. The
wrong value never became a commit.

147
00:11:33,964 --> 00:11:37,807
With auto-update on, and without
the evaluation step, it would have.

148
00:11:39,647 --> 00:11:44,752
Your turn. Do Lab 14.5,
"Rerere, resolve once", in

149
00:11:44,792 --> 00:11:49,074
lab-manual/m14-hooks-rerere-attributes.md.

150
00:11:49,414 --> 00:11:54,127
Before the second merge, predict three
things: the exit status, the output of git

151
00:11:54,167 --> 00:11:58,880
status -s, and whether the file contains
markers. The lab's failure scenario is a

152
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:03,750
wrong resolution with auto-update in a
rebase. Before you repair it, write down the

153
00:12:03,790 --> 00:12:07,858
order of the three correction steps
and what each one changes on disk.

154
00:12:08,198 --> 00:12:14,845
The challenge is Exercise 14.8, Level
3, "A resolution that nobody typed", in

155
00:12:14,885 --> 00:12:18,667
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

156
00:12:20,507 --> 00:12:22,438
Answer out loud. I'll wait.

157
00:12:27,278 --> 00:12:34,161
A strong answer names the two files and
the two moments at which they are written,

158
00:12:34,201 --> 00:12:40,915
and says what keys the record. It explains
the normalization concretely, what is

159
00:12:40,955 --> 00:12:47,332
stripped and what is sorted, and derives
the direction-independence from it.

160
00:12:47,372 --> 00:12:54,255
It states what rerere does and doesn't
touch when it replays, and what the setting

161
00:12:54,295 --> 00:13:01,348
that changes this costs. And it shows
awareness of the risk: what happens to a wrong

162
00:13:01,388 --> 00:13:05,063
resolution, and the
procedure to replace it.

163
00:13:06,903 --> 00:13:09,830
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

164
00:13:10,170 --> 00:13:15,164
Rerere stores a normalized conflict as a
preimage when the conflict appears and my

165
00:13:15,204 --> 00:13:19,462
resolution as a postimage when I
conclude, keyed by the conflict text.

166
00:13:19,802 --> 00:13:26,168
Because labels are stripped and the sides
sorted, one record serves both merge

167
00:13:26,208 --> 00:13:28,139
directions and a rebase.

168
00:13:28,479 --> 00:13:35,717
A replay writes the file only; the operation
still stops with UU, and that stop is

169
00:13:35,757 --> 00:13:36,604
my review.

170
00:13:36,944 --> 00:13:41,790
A wrong record is replaced by git rerere
forget, git restore --merge, and a new

171
00:13:41,830 --> 00:13:42,471
resolution.

172
00:13:42,811 --> 00:13:48,639
On Git 2.55 a background rerere-gc
task can collide with a rebase over

173
00:13:48,679 --> 00:13:55,514
MERGE_RR.lock; this was observed, not
documented, and maintenance.rerere-gc.auto=0

174
00:13:55,554 --> 00:13:56,352
avoids it.

175
00:13:58,192 --> 00:14:08,156
Read section 14C.3 of Chapter 14C.

176
00:14:08,496 --> 00:14:16,029
Today you watched Git reuse your own
resolution, and you know to review every

177
00:14:16,069 --> 00:14:23,897
replay. Do the rerere lab before the next
video, and read the file at each stop.

178
00:14:23,937 --> 00:14:31,568
Next time: the dot gitattributes file,
per-path settings that travel, and line

179
00:14:31,608 --> 00:14:37,567
endings. Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

180
00:14:37,607 --> 00:14:39,927
See you in the next one.
